/**

@page benchmarks_exahype2_euler_kernel_benchmarks Benchmarks of Compute Kernels for the Euler Equations

A suite of benchmark runs which assess the throughput of the Euler Finite Volume kernels.

ExaHyPE 2's implementation of the Euler equations is shipped
with a benchmark to assess the speed of the compute kernels.
The benchmark solely computes the throughput on the node or one GPU, respectively, and thus
provides key information how efficient these core kernels perform on your system.
It neglects other effects such as multithreading orchestration overhead or MPI data exchange, grid
management, time stepping behaviour, halo data exchange, and so forth.
While this might be of limited
relevance for large-scale runs, where scalability, IO overheads and other
factors play a dominant role, it tells us what the core compute routines could,
in theory, deliver. 


To run the benchmark, change into the benchmark's directory. The directory 
hosts a set of benchmarks which assess the sole performance of the individual
kernels on the CPU and GPUs. These benchmarks are shipped through Python 
scripts which should run out-of-the-box. Besides the benchmarking scripts 
themselves, there are also some postprocessing scripts which help you to 
produce fancy graphs.



# Build Benchmarks

There are different benchmarks for the different solver types (e.g., Finite
Volumes (FV) and Discontinuous Galerkin (DG)) and you have to build different executables
for different patch sizes, orders or dimensions.


## Finite Volumes Rusanov

To build the executables you can run following commands:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    export PYTHONPATH=../../../python

    python3 kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov.py --dim 2 --patch-size 4
    python3 kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov.py --dim 2 --patch-size 6
    python3 kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov.py --dim 2 --patch-size 8

    python3 kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov.py --dim 3 --patch-size 4
    python3 kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov.py --dim 3 --patch-size 6
    python3 kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov.py --dim 3 --patch-size 8
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

## Cleanup

You can throw away all of the glue code after a successful build.
This is triggered by:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    make clean
    make distclean
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

If you want to keep the executable, you will need to rename it.


## Run Benchmarks

All the benchmarks run out of the box without any user interaction once built.
All of them produce outputs in a similar way, so it is easier to interpret the
outcomes. For most runs, you don't have to specify anything. 

However, OpenMP, SYCL and other back-ends might want you to configure your 
environment in specific ways. You might have to set some environment 
variables specifying the GPUs to use or core counts accordingly.
The exact usage obviously depends strongly @ref page_compiler_specific_settings "on your software stack".

## Example for complete test pipeline

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
# Clonning the repo
git clone -b gpus-sycl-siam https://gitlab.lrz.de/hpcsoftware/Peano


# Autotools command
cd Peano libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .

automake --add-missing


# To use with Intel LLVM
./configure CC=clang CXX=clang++ CXXFLAGS="-O3 -fsycl -fsycl-targets=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda -Xsycl-target-backend=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda --cuda-gpu-arch=sm_80" LIBS="-ltbb" LDFLAGS="-fsycl -fsycl-targets=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda -Xsycl-target-backend=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda --cuda-gpu-arch=sm_80"  --with-multithreading=tbb --enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured --with-gpu=sycl 


# To use with Intel proprietary compiler
./configure CC=icx CXX=icpx LIBS=-ltbb LDFLAGS="-fsycl -g -tbb" CXXFLAGS="-tbb -O3 -fsycl" -with-multithreading=tbb -enable-exahype -enable-blockstructured -enable-loadbalancing -with-gpu=sycl 


# Build the benchmark (with d=3 and p=8)
cd benchmarks/exahype2/euler/kernel-benchmarks

export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python

python3 kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov.py --dim 3 --patch-size 8


#Run and plot the timings for AoS batched kernel kernel (select your device with --gpus, see available devices by entering sycl-ls to the terminal)
for i in 8; do
	./kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov-3d-patch-size-$i --threads 1 --gpus 2 > sycl-3d-ps$i.txt
done

python3 create-exahype-sycl-plot.py -f sycl-3d-ps8.txt -kv batched -t 50 -ds AoS -ps 8 
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


# Outputs

### Finite Volumes Rusanov
You can run the benchmarks as follows:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    ./kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov-2d-patch-size-4 --threads 1 -gpus all
    ./kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov-2d-patch-size-4 --threads 2 -gpus all
    ./kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov-2d-patch-size-4 --threads 4 -gpus all
    ./kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov-2d-patch-size-4 --threads 8 -gpus all
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

to obtain a first insight into how the throughput changes as more and more
threads hit the GPUs with kernel invocations.

A typical output reads as follows:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
========================================
  Number of patches fused: 4
 ========================================
 baseline: 0.000141742 / 2.88504e-08
 stateless kernels on host with heap, patch-wise, AoS: 0.000156606 / 3.18758e-08
 stateless kernels on host with heap, patch-wise, AoSoA: 0.000114747 / 2.33558e-08
 stateless kernels on host with heap, patch-wise, SoA: 0.000114582 / 2.33222e-08
 stateless kernels on host with heap, patch-wise, in-situ: 3.5338e-05 / 7.19275e-09
 stateless kernels on host with heap, batched, AoS: 0.000116653 / 2.37437e-08
 stateless kernels on host with heap, batched, AoSoA: 0.000115959 / 2.36025e-08
 stateless kernels on host with heap, batched, SoA: 0.000114284 / 2.32616e-08
 stateless kernels on host with heap, batched, in-situ:  \end{terminal}
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The code takes multiple patches (here 4) and hands them over to one compute
kernel, i.e. one compute kernel processes N patches in one rush. The code
runs a baseline experiment first. This is the most generic numerical kernel
that ExaHyPE 2 offers, and one that supports all kinds of side-effects and 
polymorphism. After that, the benchmark assesses the speed of various kernel
variants and gives you both the total runtime and the runtime per degree of
freedom.



If you want to test your system with OpenMP offloading,
e.g., you will need an additional `–with-gpu=omp`. Though the
benchmarks in principle assess only the core performance of the
compute kernels, they can mimick the concurrent offloading from
multiple threads. To enable this feature, you have to activate
multithreading, too (add `–with-multithreading=omp` for example).
Run `./configure –help` for various combinations of GPU and
multithreading back-ends. Finally, you will have to add additional
flags on particular system. In particular the flags `CXX`, and
`LDFLAGS` to pick your compiler, you compiler settings and your
linker parameters. Chapter
[\[chapter:selected-HPC-platforms\]](#chapter:selected-HPC-platforms){reference-type="ref"
reference="chapter:selected-HPC-platforms"} discusses some popular
choices. Skip this step if you have already completed it once.

The Python script generating and the benchmark supports different
options and benchmark settings. Type in `–help` for additional
information. The statement above assesses the kernel performance if
only one thread is used. Offloading to the GPUs from multiple
threads is notoriously tricky, while running a kernel benchmark on
multiple cores stresses the interconnect. I therefore always
recommend to start with the single core measurements. Finally,
there's a `kernel-benchmark.md` markup file which describes further
settings and also discusses built-in postprocessing options.

The benchmark gives you the total runtime and cost per degree of freedom
update (throughput) on one core or GPU, respectively. Data is provided
for various patch sizes batched (fused) into one large patch assembly,
for patches which exploit stateless operations, and different kernel
realisations.

Baseline is what you get with most basic configuration and its
flexible architecture, where users can tailor each PDE term towards
their needs. The other variants exploit hints how to tune these
computations and try out various optimisations, algorithmic
reformulations and internal data formats for temporary data. Please note
that we assess only the Euler equations here, i.e. we are extremely
bandwidth-bound and induce almost no load. All GPU data comprise all
data transfer and memory allocations on the GPU. They provide fair
measurements and not raw compute figures. We have data for SYCL,
OpenMP and C++  GPU benchmarks.


## Visualisation of Results
If you want to use the pre-manufactured visualisation scripts, you have to pipe
the program output into a file, i.e.,

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    python3 create-scaling-plot.py outputfile
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

*/
